Ambassador Dan Negrea
Representative of the United States to the UN Economic and Social Council
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you for the briefing. We welcome the opportunity to discuss the protection of medical care in armed conflict.
With the adoption of UN Security Council resolution 2286, the UN Security Council demanded that the international community mobilize in an effort to prevent attacks in armed conflict on medical personnel and humanitarian personnel exclusively engaged in medical duties, and urged States to ensure accountability for those responsible for such attacks.
However, on this tenth anniversary of the resolution, a staggering number of attacks on health facilities, health workers, ambulances, and patients continue to take place across the globe.
The United States strongly supports efforts to promote access to humanitarian relief, including medical care, for civilians in situations of armed conflict. We recognize the dangers health workers often find themselves in and express our appreciation to these brave individuals.
The United States condemns in the strongest terms attacks conducted on hospitals and, at times, patients in Sudan. Hospitals have been destroyed by both the RSF and SAF, cutting off lifesaving services for hundreds of thousands of vulnerable civilians already facing acute malnutrition. There have also been reports of massacres of health workers and patients in Sudan.
Troublingly, the Russia-Ukraine war has also endangered medical care, as strikes against hospitals and other civilian infrastructure—like those in southern Odesa on April 29—persist.
We condemn in the strongest possible terms Houthi detentions of international aid staff, many of whom have dedicated their lives to providing life-sustaining medical aid to the people of Yemen who desperately need it. The abhorrent actions by the Houthis only further isolate Yemen and deprive Yemenis of the critical services they need.
The United States calls for an end to impunity for such violations of international humanitarian law and looks forward to working with the members of the Security Council in implementing resolution 2286.
Thank you.
